The product rules for $e$ and $ell$ give

$
  Gamma_alpha (x y) = ee(alpha ell(x) + alpha ell(y)) = Gamma_alpha (x) Gamma_alpha (y), quad Gamma_(alpha + beta) (x) = ee((alpha + beta) ell(x)) = Gamma_alpha (x) Gamma_beta (x),
$

and composing recovers multiplication of exponents,

$
  Gamma_alpha (Gamma_beta (x)) = ee(alpha ell(e(beta ell(x)))) = ee(alpha beta ell(x)) = Gamma_(alpha beta)(x),
$

while $Gamma_1 (x) = x$ and $Gamma_0 (x) = 1$. For integers, repeated products give $Gamma_p (x) = x^p$ and, via $Gamma_(alpha beta) = Gamma_alpha compose Gamma_beta$, $Gamma_(-p) (x) = x^(-p)$. For $q in NN$, $(Gamma_(1\/q) (x))^q = Gamma_1 (x) = x$ forces $Gamma_(1\/q) (x) = x^(1\/q)$, hence $x^(p\/q) = Gamma_(p\/q) (x)$: on rational exponents $Gamma_alpha$ agrees with ordinary powers.
